 --> 
 Jitendra Gohil, Bajaj Alts 
 India's GDP may near 8% growth, but large-cap profits lag.
 Democratisation of capital boosts new-age firms and AIFs.
 Pharma remains overweight while large-caps face pressure.
 Did our AI summary help? 
 Market Mastery
India’s economy could grow closer to 8%, but profit growth at large listed companies will stay muted, says Jitendra Gohil, chief investment officer for listed equities at Bajaj Alts. The RBI raised its FY27 growth forecast recently to 7.1%. With more than two decades in the industry (including stints as Chief Investment Strategist at Kotak Alts and heading the India Investment Committee at Credit Suisse Wealth Management), Gohil has built a framework he calls “Connecting the Dots.” It explicitly links politics, policy, macro shifts and stock selection. He explains how this lens has reshaped his view of India, why corporate earnings may lag GDP, and where Bajaj Alts is positioning its PMS and Category-III AIF strategies.
 How has your framework for looking at India evolved? 
Global developments have become the bigger driving force for Indian markets. We no longer look at India purely through a traditional domestic-macro lens. Pure macro analysis rarely makes money. The more useful middle path is politics: how politics shapes macro outcomes and how the two together shape equity markets. At Bajaj Alts we study global forces along with macro and policy shifts to identify sector rotations and multi-bagger opportunities.
A useful illustration is the China–India comparison from 2000 to 2026. China’s per-capita income rose roughly 15 times while India’s rose 5 to 6 times. Yet the Chinese equity market delivered only about half the returns of the Indian market. The difference lies in political structure. India’s democratic system has allowed a relatively concentrated set of companies to drive market returns even while the broader macro economy remained comparatively fragile. India’s per-capita income is still near $3,000, yet the equity market has delivered strong long-term returns.
 What is the most important structural shift over the last decade? 
Democratisation of capital. After the post-2009 global liquidity surge, capital became cheaper and more widely available through private credit and private funding. Combined with the democratisation of information and technology, this has enabled younger companies to raise significant capital and challenge established players. We are seeing a David-versus-Goliath dynamic across several categories.
India now has roughly 391 dollar billionaires, ranking third globally. This newly created wealth, together with the ongoing financialisation of household savings, is why we are expanding into PMS and AIFs. AIF commitments have reached Rs 17.5 trillion as of June 2026, with cumulative funds raised exceeding Rs 7.1 trillion.
 How do you see the gold and household-savings opportunity? 
Indian households hold more than 25,000 tonnes of gold, three times the roughly 8,000 tonnes held by the US Treasury, currently valued at over $3.5 trillion. India’s bank credit-to-GDP ratio stands at around 50 to 52%, while the developed-market average is about 150%. That gap is the runway. The gold-loan book has surged 145% in the past three years. Organised gold-loan outstanding is still only a single-digit percentage of the household stock, so monetisation remains early.
Net household financial savings as a percentage of GNDI have started to improve. Structural factors include shrinking family sizes, a total fertility rate of 1.9 (below the replacement rate of 2.1), and reduced pressure to buy housing immediately. Indians are prioritising spending over saving. Combined centre and state subsidies and social spending could reach roughly Rs 15.5 trillion, up from Rs 6.7 trillion in FY19. This, along with easier access to finance, supports consumption.
 The RBI has just raised its FY27 GDP forecast to 7.1%. How do you see growth versus corporate earnings? 
The Indian economy may still surprise on the upside and move closer to 8% growth. Gross fixed capital formation reached 34.3% of GDP in the June 2026 quarter, up from 31.4% in the previous financial year. Bank credit growth has accelerated to 19.1% YoY, the highest since 2011 excluding one-off effects, while gross NPAs are at a multi-decade low of 2.2%. Gross FDI inflows hit a record of about $95 billion last year.
However, I remain cautious on corporate earnings, especially for large-caps. Competition from well-funded new businesses is challenging incumbents. Companies are cash-rich. A meaningful share of recent IPO proceeds has gone towards debt repayment or promoter OFS rather than pure expansion. Firms are also entering unrelated businesses, which tends to compress margins. High ROEs, low leverage and rising competition create a stampede into existing bus

