Considering the weak broader market structure, global uncertainties, elevated crude oil prices, and persistent FII outflows, the Nifty 50's upside is likely to remain capped, at least in the short term. In terms of key levels, 22,300 could act as immediate support, followed by 22,180, a crucial swing-low support. A decisive break below the latter could intensify selling pressure. On the upside, the 22,500–22,600 zone is likely to act as immediate resistance, followed by 22,800 as a critical hurdle for further gains. Meanwhile, the Bank Nifty needs to move decisively above its 20-day EMA at 55,425 to extend its recovery towards the 56,200–56,500 resistance zone. On the downside, 54,400 could act as immediate support, followed by 54,000, experts said.
On October 12, the Nifty 50 fell 157 points, or 0.70 percent, to close at 22,363, while the Bank Nifty declined 339 points, or 0.61 percent, to end at 54,918. Market breadth on the NSE favoured declines, with around 1,705 stocks falling compared with 1,580 stocks advancing.
 Jay Thakkar, Vice President & Head of Derivatives and Quant Research at ICICI Securities 
The Nifty has once again slipped into negative territory after failing to sustain the recovery seen on Friday, when the index snapped its eight-week losing streak. This indicated that selling pressure continues to dominate despite the recent relief rally. The failure to sustain above 22,600 level suggests that buying interest remains weak at higher levels.
On the downside, the immediate support zone is placed at 22,300–22,200 levels, with 22,180 acting as a crucial swing low. A decisive breach of this zone could trigger another round of selling and drag the index towards 22,000 and subsequently 21,700 levels. On the upside, 22,500 will act as immediate resistance, followed by 22,800, which is crucial for any meaningful recovery. Unless the index reclaims and sustains above 22,800, the short-term trend is likely to remain sideways to negative.
The broader market structure continues to remain weak, with global uncertainties, elevated crude oil prices and persistent foreign investor outflows weighing on sentiment. Although the recent recovery indicated some easing in selling pressure, Monday's reversal suggests that the market has yet to establish a sustainable bottom. Hence, traders should remain cautious and look for selling opportunities on rallies rather than anticipate a sustained recovery at current levels.
 Strategy : Sell Nifty Futures on rise near 22,500 with a stop-loss of above 22,800 and target of 22,200 and 22,000, or buy on dips near 22,000 with a stop-loss below 21,700 and target of 22,500 and 22,800.
 Jigar S Patel, Senior Manager - Equity Research at Anand Rathi 
The Nifty is trading near a crucial long-term support zone of 22,180, where the April and October 2026 lows indicate a potential double-bottom formation. The monthly RSI has improved from 43.67 to 44.37 despite similar price levels, signaling a possible bullish divergence and weakening selling momentum.
Additionally, a three-point bullish divergence on the daily chart strengthens the possibility of a recovery. The monthly RSI is holding within the historical 40–45 support zone, while the 50-month SMA at 22,326 and the rising long-term trendline provide further technical support. Thus, we expect 22,200 support to hold on closing basis.
 Strategy : Buy Nifty Futures on dip with a stop-loss of 22,250, and target of 22,800.
 Somil Mehta, Head of Retail Research at Mirae Asset ShareKhan 
Nifty ended the week in positive territory, forming a Doji candlestick after eight straight weeks of losses. Even so, the index is still below its key daily and hourly averages, which suggests that the weakness may continue in the short term. On the upside, Monday’s high of 22,650 will be the immediate resistance level.
The bears will stay in charge of the short-term trend until the index is trading below 22,800. On the downside, 22,182 is the first support, and a close below it can open the way to 21,750.
 Strategy : Sell Nifty Futures at CMP of 22,399.90 with a stop-loss of 22,800 on a closing basis, and target of 22,182 – 21,750.
