After rebounding from oversold levels, with a Doji formation on the weekly chart and a Bullish Harami pattern on the daily chart, market sentiment has somewhat improved. However, the Nifty 50 needs strong follow-up buying to move decisively above 23,000, as the broader technical structure remains weak. Until then, consolidation and range-bound trading may continue, with immediate support at 22,500, followed by the crucial support level of 22,180. On the upside, immediate resistance is seen at 22,600, followed by 22,800, according to experts. Meanwhile, the Bank Nifty needs to move above its 20-day EMA at 55,500 to extend its rally towards the resistance level of 56,200. On the downside, immediate support is placed at 55,000, followed by the 54,600–54,400 zone.
On October 9, the Nifty 50 rose 289 points, or 1.30 percent, to close at 22,520, while the Bank Nifty gained 742 points, or 1.36 percent, to end at 55,257. Market breadth turned positive, with around 1,978 stocks advancing against 1,267 declining on the NSE.
 Osho Krishan, Chief Manager - Technical & Derivative Research at Angel One 
After eight consecutive weeks of sustained selling pressure, which dragged the benchmark index down by more than 10 percent and pushed key technical indicators into deeply oversold territory, the bulls finally staged a recovery, bringing the prolonged losing streak to an end. While the broader technical structure remains weak, with the formation of lower bottoms indicating a persistent bearish trend, the index is approaching a crucial support zone that coincides with the lows recorded in April 2026 and April 2025.
Additionally, the formation of a Doji candlestick on the weekly chart offers a glimmer of hope for a potential relief rally. However, the overall market outlook remains cautious, as a decisive breakout above the previous swing high is still required to signal a meaningful trend reversal.
From a technical perspective, the 22,200–22,000 zone emerges as a crucial support area. A decisive breach below this range could further weaken the near-term market structure, potentially accelerating selling pressure towards the 21,750–21,700 levels. On the upside, immediate resistance is placed around 22,750–22,800, followed by the psychological 23,000 mark. A sustained move above these resistance zones would be essential to provide near-term relief and revive buying momentum.
 Strategy : Buy Nifty October Futures near 22,300, with a stop-loss at 22,000 and a profit-booking target of 22,800–23,000.
 Jatin Gedia, Technical Research Analyst, VP, Derivatives & Technical Research at Teji Mandi 
On the weekly chart, the Nifty formed a Bullish Harami Cross, a two-candlestick reversal pattern that appears at the bottom of a downtrend. It signals that selling pressure may be easing and a reversal of the prevailing downtrend is possible. More importantly, the pattern has formed in the support cluster of 22,200–22,400, which coincides with the 200-week exponential moving average (EMA) and the rising support trendline drawn by connecting the previous two swing lows of 21,743 in April 2025 and 22,182 in April 2026.
A positive divergence, along with oversold readings on the Relative Strength Index (RSI), suggests a high probability of a counter-trend rally towards 22,800, the previous swing high. A move above 22,800 could signal a reversal of the downtrend and trigger a short-covering rally towards 23,000–23,170. On the other hand, a breach below 22,180 could lead to a decline towards the psychological support level of 22,000.
Open-interest data also indicate bullish positioning, with significant Put writing at the 22,500, 22,400 and 22,300 strikes, alongside the unwinding of Call positions at the 22,400 strike.
 Strategy : Buy Nifty October Futures around 22,618, with a stop-loss at 22,440 and a target of 22,840–22,950.
 Vaishnavi Jagtap, Senior Research Analyst – Technical at Axis Direct 
